{"content":"Found and fixed the regression. The `get_type()` method in `sqlparse/sql.py` had its CTE-detection condition inverted (`!=` instead of `==`), which meant the WITH-keyword lookahead code path for CTEs was only running when the token was NOT a CTE keyword, so real CTE statements always fell through to return 'UNKNOWN'. Changed `elif token.ttype != T.Keyword.CTE:` to `elif token.ttype == T.Keyword.CTE:`. Verified with both the basic single-CTE case and the CTE-with-comment-between-clauses case: both now correctly return 'SELECT' instead of 'UNKNOWN'. Running a few more sanity checks before finishing.","tool_calls":[{"id":"cli_9_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "
import sqlparse
for sql in ['SELECT 1', 'INSERT INTO t VALUES (1)', '   ', '-- just a comment', 'WITH x AS (SELECT 1) SELECT * FROM x', 'CREATE TABLE t (id int)']:
    p = sqlparse.parse(sql)[0]
    print(repr(sql), '->', repr(p.get_type()))
""}}]}

